<title>USA: U.S. June producer prices fell 0.1 pct from May.</title>
<headline>U.S. June producer prices fell 0.1 pct from May.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>   Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p>				  June    May	June97/96</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     -0.1   -0.3	     -0.1</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1   -0.3		0.2</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	    -0.9    0.4		0.3</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		 0.7   -2.1	     -1.3</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  131.6  131.5		n/a</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     June    May	June97/96</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     0.4   -7.1	     -5.9</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		 -5.8    2.2		1.3</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	   0.1    0.4		3.6</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	     0.3   -1.6	     -2.4</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	  0.1   -0.2	     -0.1</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	 Unch   -0.2	     -0.4</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1   Unch		0.1</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	 0.1   Unch	     -0.4</p>
<p> -Construction Materials  -0.1    0.4		2.2</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p>The 0.1 percent drop in the June PPI was a record sixth consecutive monthly decline. The previous record was the five-month string from August to December 1952.</p>
<p>The 14.1 percent drop in crude petroleum prices was the largest monthly decline since a 15.4 percent fall in December 1993, the department said.</p>
</text>
